Here are a few polished options depending on your preferred style:

### Option 1: Elevated & Fluent (Best for scientific papers or technical descriptions)

> A (meth)acryloyl compound represented by the following formula (A):  
> wherein **X** is a single bond or a 2m-valent group containing 1 to 60 carbon atoms; each **Z** is independently an oxygen atom, a sulfur atom, or does not form a crosslink; each **R¹** is independently selected from a linear, branched, or cyclic alkyl group having 1 to 30 carbon atoms, or an aryl group (C₆–C₃₀), alkenyl group (C₂–C₃₀), or alkoxy group (C₁–C₃₀), each optionally bearing a substituent; a halogen atom; a nitro group; an amino group; a carboxylic acid group; a thiol group; a hydroxy group; or a hydroxy group wherein a hydrogen atom is replaced with a vinylphenylmethyl group, wherein any such alkyl, aryl, alkenyl, or alkoxy group optionally incorporates an ether, ketone, or ester bond; each **R¹ᴬ** is independently a hydrogen atom or a methyl group; each **k** is independently an integer from 0 to 2, provided that not all **k** moieties are simultaneously 0; **m** is an integer from 1 to 3; each **n** is independently an integer from 0 to 5; and each **p** is independently 0 or 1.

---

### Option 2: Structured & Clear (Best for clarity and formal patent/legal specifications)

> A (meth)acryloyl compound represented by formula (A):  
> 
> * **X** is a single bond or a 2m-valent group having 1 to 60 carbon atoms;  
> * each **Z** is independently an oxygen atom, a sulfur atom, or not a crosslink;  
> * each **R¹** is independently:
>   * a linear, branched, or cyclic alkyl group having 1 to 30 carbon atoms;  
>   * an aryl group (6 to 30 carbon atoms), an alkenyl group (2 to 30 carbon atoms), or an alkoxy group (1 to 30 carbon atoms), each optionally having a substituent;  
>   * a halogen atom, a nitro group, an amino group, a carboxylic acid group, a thiol group, or a hydroxy group; or  
>   * a group in which a hydrogen atom of a hydroxy group is replaced with a vinylphenylmethyl group;  
>   *(wherein said alkyl, aryl, alkenyl, or alkoxy groups optionally contain an ether, ketone, or ester bond);*  
> * each **R¹ᴬ** is independently a methyl group or a hydrogen atom;  
> * each **k** is independently an integer of 0 to 2, with the proviso that not all **k** moieties are simultaneously 0;  
> * **m** is an integer of 1 to 3;  
> * each **n** is independently an integer of 0 to 5; and  
> * each **p** is independently 0 or 1.

---

### Key Improvements Made:
* **Reduced Repetition:** Combined repetitive phrases like *"optionally having a substituent"* into a streamlined list.
* **Refined Phrasing:** Changed *"all of the k moieties are not 0 at the same time"* to the standard technical expression *"not all k moieties are simultaneously 0"*.
* **Improved Flow:** Replaced heavy phrasing (e.g., *"a group in which a hydrogen atom of a hydroxy group is replaced with..."*) with smoother transitions without losing exact legal/chemical meaning.

